  number={},
  pages={911-915},
  abstract={In appearance-based gaze estimation, the generalization performance of the model is limited by various factors in the image that are not directly related to gaze. We link the generalization issue of gaze estimation models with the hallucination problem in visual models. We propose the Visual Contrastive Guidance architecture for generalized gaze estimation (VCG-Gaze), which enhances the model’s ability to extract gaze-critical features by constructing contrastive samples during the model learning process. The construction of contrastive samples draws on Visual Contrastive Decoding technique for hallucination mitigation of Vision-Language Models. VCG-Gaze employs a multi-branch Encoder-Decoder structure to parallelly process the gaze image along with its contrastive sample, and then the core gaze-critical features get extracted for gaze prediction. We conduct extensive experiments to demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed method and its components. Results show that the proposed method not only achieves state-of-the-art performance among generalized models, but also shows strong competitiveness compared with domain adaptation methods. Code has been made publicly available at: https://github.com/Corsilent/VCG-Gaze.},
